# End-to-end tests

These run {{ .ProviderName }} against a kind control plane with Crossplane
installed and the provider deployed from a locally built package.

```
test/
├── setup.sh            # runs once before the tests; applies the ProviderConfigs
├── e2e/                # resource lifecycle, generated by uptest
│   └── <kind>-lifecycle.yaml   # test input; its annotations configure uptest
└── behavior/           # controller behaviour, plain chainsaw tests
    └── <kind>-pause/   # seed test: pausing must actually stop reconciliation
```

**`e2e/`** is the managed resource lifecycle — create, observe, delete.
[uptest](https://github.com/crossplane/uptest) generates it from the annotations
on each `*-lifecycle.yaml`, because that lifecycle is the same for every managed
resource. Every `create api` adds a lifecycle file for the new kind; the
Makefile picks them up by wildcard, so nothing needs wiring.

**`behavior/`** is everything else — drift, pause, credential resolution, error
paths. uptest has no vocabulary for these, so they are plain
[chainsaw](https://kyverno.github.io/chainsaw/) tests.

## Running

```bash
make e2e                # full flow: build package → kind + Crossplane →
                        # deploy provider → uptest lifecycles → chainsaw behavior
make test-behavior      # chainsaw behavior tests only, against a live cluster
make test-integration   # fast loop: controller from source, no packaging
```

`make e2e UPTEST_SKIP_DELETE=true` leaves resources behind to inspect them.
`make uptest UPTEST_RENDER_ONLY=true` renders the generated lifecycle steps
without running them — reach for it before debugging a failure, the generated
step is often not what the annotations suggest.

Each command uses its own kind cluster and prints the name it acts on;
`make help` lists them.

## Adding a test

```bash
xp-provider-gen create-test              # prompts for name and kind
xp-provider-gen create-test --name drift-check --kind MyKind
```

That scaffolds `test/behavior/<name>/chainsaw-test.yaml` with an apply→assert
skeleton to build on. For custom lifecycle assertions, add uptest hook
annotations (`uptest.upbound.io/post-assert-hook`, …) to a lifecycle file —
see the [upstream provider-template](https://github.com/crossplane/provider-template/tree/main/test)
for worked hook examples.

These files are yours: the generator seeds them once and never overwrites them.
